# What CI scans the TRACKED files against. Generated, not hand-written: this is
# .secretlintignore truncated at its local-only sentinel, and nothing else.
#
# That file skips a developer's own untracked .env so `pnpm verify` does not fail
# on the file README.md just told them to write. This one asks the different
# question CI is for, which is whether a dotenv file is in git at all.
# `check:secrets:tracked` feeds it `git ls-files`, so only a file somebody
# committed can reach it, and a force-added, web-edited or bot-authored .env is
# refused where the working-tree scan would wave it through.
#
# scripts/ci/secretlint-ignore.test.mjs holds the two files to exactly this
# relationship, so an exemption added below the sentinel cannot silently reach
# CI, and one added above it is visible in review as reaching both.

node_modules/
pnpm-lock.yaml
dist/
build/
.next/
coverage/
.turbo/
*.png
*.jpg
*.jpeg
*.gif
*.svg
*.ico
*.woff
*.woff2

# Everything below this line is skipped for a developer's working tree and NOT
# for CI. `.secretlintignore.ci` is this file truncated here, and
# `check:secrets:tracked` scans what git tracks against that, so an exemption
# added below can only ever excuse an untracked local file.
